summary:
I mapped how models are run today and found one model server on one machine. LlamaServerManager holds at most one process and ModelManager has one current_id. start_sidecar always stops the previous model before it starts the next. guard_agent_running refuses a switch while any chat or agent run is active anywhere. Chat, agent, compaction and benchmark requests carry no model id: they take the base_url and settings of whatever model is running (api.rs:3452, agent_runner.rs:2619).

Answers to the six questions:

(1) Places that assume one loaded model: LlamaServerManager, ModelManager (current, switch_to, unload_all), the stub mirror engine in AppState.inference, the single global LoadProgress, FitPreparation (which runs only when nothing is loaded), and the fixed port 3888. Also these handlers: load_model, load_model_by_id, prepare_conversation, inference_start, inference_stop, unload_models, recheck_model_tooling, benchmark, runtime_policy, system_overview, cancel_load and calibrate_model. Finally, the process-wide chars-per-token estimate is reset on every load. All of this is replaced by three things:
- a model registry and a table of model copies in PostgreSQL, owned by the model pool manager;
- a gateway call made with a model id and a conversation id;
- draining one copy at a time instead of the global "no runs anywhere" guard.

(2) Tool-ability check: tooling.rs measures one loaded llama.cpp model with 2 to 4 probe requests. It writes tooling.json beside the weights, keyed by the runtime build (BUILD_INFO.json) and a fingerprint of the chat template. To become an ability profile it needs:
- storage in a database table instead of the models folder, keyed by model id plus model file hash, plus runtime build or provider and model version, plus template fingerprint;
- context size, vision support and the template's capabilities in the same record;
- runs as a queued worker job through the gateway, so vLLM and outside providers can be checked too;
- tool definitions taken from tool and plugin description files instead of the hard-coded table in tooling::parameters.

(3) Sidecar HTTP client. These parts are already a generic OpenAI-format client: the messages and tool-call format, streaming (SSE) parsing including streamed tool-call fragments, error status mapping, the reasoning_content field, usage counts, the retry-once-on-connect logic and the request recorder. These parts are llama.cpp-specific:
- request fields cache_prompt, top_k, repeat_penalty, timings_per_token, chat_template_kwargs, and the response_format of type json_object with a schema;
- the /props template capabilities, /tokenize, the timings object and the exceed_context_size_error type;
- the --parallel 1 single slot and the template-refusal retry.

(4) Memory fit, calibration, hardware profiling and downloads. The runtime fit (llama-fit-params), the policy, drafting and memory-mapping choices, CPU fallback, cpu_topology and the speed rule all still apply on a GPU server, inside the pool manager's process for each node. But they need a GPU index for each copy, VRAM already reserved by other copies, and multiplication by slots. Only the battery-or-mains check and the "close other GPU-heavy applications" notes are laptop-only. Calibration, model import/scan, downloads, delete, tool re-check and benchmark become admin dashboard actions, run by a worker or the pool manager. The daio and recommend heuristics, auto_configure, describe_model and cache_budget are superseded by the runtime fit and can be removed.

(5) Resource meters and system endpoints. Each process keeps an in-memory one-hour sample buffer and runs nvidia-smi (busiest card only). With many servers, each GPU node reports per-GPU readings to a central store, and the admin dashboard reads them. Users keep only the meters for their own request: context, tokens per second and cache hits.

(6) Routing the same conversation to the same model copy:
- each copy has one slot, so any other conversation evicts the cache;
- llama.cpp picks a slot by prompt similarity and the client never sends id_slot;
- adding slots divides the context window, and the fit does not account for that;
- the RAM prompt cache belongs to each copy;
- the run's side requests (completion check, compaction) also need the same copy;
- an agent run captures one copy's address at start and fails instead of moving if that copy dies;
- the token-estimate ratio is process-global.

The affinity map (conversation or run to copy) must live in a shared store, with a fallback that re-reads the prompt.

items.change:
This becomes the 'local processes' backend of the pool manager:
- each copy gets its own free port, its own log file and a registry row (node, address, state, started time, last error);
- the process handle stays on the machine that owns it;
- a Kubernetes backend does the same through a Deployment and a readiness check on /health.
The API servers and workers never hold process handles.

items.what_it_does:
Adds request fields only llama.cpp understands:
- a flag that keeps the prompt cached;
- two extra sampling knobs;
- timing data on every streamed piece;
- a switch for the chat template's thinking mode;
- a JSON-schema format in llama.cpp's own shape.
It also asks the server what the chat template supports (/props) and counts tokens with /tokenize.

items.change:
Split into three:
- a launch specification owned by the pool manager and set by the admin for each deployment;
- sampling options resolved for each user and request;
- the ability profile for each model, read from the database.
Remove the default local file path.

items.what_it_does:
The whole model-load pipeline:
- stops the previous model and marks everything unloaded;
- measures GPU memory and RAM;
- applies the user's settings, the calibration profile and the runtime fit, then chooses memory-mapping and the prompt-cache size;
- starts the server with GPU-then-CPU fallback;
- reads the template's capabilities, runs the tool check, and publishes the model as current.

surprises.why_it_matters:
Several things make 'same conversation to the same copy' a requirement:
- the client never says which slot to use, so llama.cpp picks by prompt similarity;
- the RAM prompt cache belongs to each copy;
- completion checks and compaction notes are deliberately built on the run's own prompt (including the tool list) so they hit the cache.
So the affinity map (conversation or agent run to copy) must sit in a shared store that every stateless API server and worker reads. There must be a fallback when a copy is drained or dies, at the cost of re-reading the prompt. The app's stable-prefix ordering helps both llama.cpp and vLLM prefix caching.

These steps assume nothing else is using the GPU:
- the fit preparation runs only when no model is loaded;
- calibration stops the running model;
- the policy counts all other GPU use as foreign;
- notes tell the user to close other GPU-heavy applications;
- GPU memory is read from nvidia-smi only, keeping the busiest card;
- no option ever pins a copy to a GPU.
Several copies or models on a multi-GPU server need GPU assignment per copy, memory reserved for copies that are starting, and one start at a time per GPU so concurrent fits cannot double-book memory.
